    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Capacitive pressure sensors have generated significant interest for applications in human health and performance monitoring. A wide range of potential applications require low cost, compliant sensors that can be tuned to appropriate sensing regimes and sensitivities. Previous literature has shown that microstructuring the dielectric layers of sensors can provide significant increases in sensitivity and that dielectric geometry can directly impact sensor range and performance. Leveraging advances in high resolution 3D printing we have developed a new method for fabricating elastomeric lattices with highly tunable geometric properties for use as capacitive pressure sensor dielectrics. We characterized the impact of microstructure geometry on the capacitive pressure sensors’ mechanical performance and developed a new set of analytical equations suitable for the lattice geometries to predict the capacitance response and sensitives of microstructured sensors under external pressure. This work covers 1) design rationale of lattice unit cells, 2) an analytical expression that directly predicts the capacitance response, and 3) 3D printing elastomeric lattice dielectric layers, which taken together serve as a framework and starting point to design and fabricate capacitive pressure sensors for various biomedical applications as well as for human health and performance monitoring.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Stein, E. (2023). 3D Printing Lattice Based Pressure Sensors for Human Health and Performance Monitoring. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/tf083kr3437. https://doi.org/10.25740/tf083kr3437.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
